front and calls attention to the  cuts but instead of disrupting the film  it lends a rhythm to these scenes and it  gives more information to the viewer in  a shorter period of time soon after her  films received acclaim editors  throughout the industry mimicked Allen's  signature fast paced editing and her  techniques became commonplace another  example of an editing technique that she  made standard was pre lapping audio from  the next scene into the scene preceding  [Music]  these are techniques so widely used now  it almost seems mundane but at the time  it was another example of her  breaking style at the start of Serpico  she uses it to great effect we hear the  siren the rhythmic beating of the  windshield and the labored breathing  before we even see a character on screen  she communicated the tense tone of the  film immediately and it works this  opening challenge is several established  editing conventions he said you always  start with a long shot then you move in  to an over shoulder or a group shot and  he said and you never changed that order  and somehow or other I was breaking  constantly instead of opening on a wide  shot of the city or even a medium shot  of the car Allen chose to open the film  on a close-up of Al Pacino's bleeding  face she replaced a spatial establishing  shot with a tonal establishing shot and  that combined with the pre lapping  sounds Prime's the audience for the  tense and unsettling tone of the film  its innovations like hers that have  become so ingrained in film language  that we still see her influence  everywhere Allen's preference to start a  scene even a film on a close-up of a  character reveals something very  important about her style her focus on  performance she was an actor's editor  and often kept in mistakes as long as  they gave authenticity to the scene okay  while her editing was no doubt in her  face and never sacrificed the moments  the camera captured we can see her  ability to capture the moment in the  famous Attica scene in dog day afternoon  the editing his stylistic and the cuts  speed up overtime but she still takes  the time to hold on Pacino as he paces  nervously and considers his situation or  to cut back to his partners nervous look  inside the bank considering the scale of  this scene with interiors exteriors  several characters and even aerial shots  it would have been no surprise if it had  to sacrifice these intimate moments but  Allen's care to build naturally and  focus on character rather than spectacle  makes it such an electric moment these  innovations are why she became the first  editor woman or man to receive an  opening title card exclusively for her  embodying Clyde in a position that  originally left the female editor  invisible her style not only enhanced  visual storytelling but it clearly shows  the influence and editor has on the  final film  by the 1980s TV Allen's talent became  well-established and later in her career  she had a role in more than just  post-production while editing for The  Breakfast Club writer director John  Hughes referred to her as his film  teacher she would regularly contribute  during filming and she was there in  person with all her equipment she would  walk onto the set and tell John if there  was a shot or an angle she could already  see when she would cut together what we  were doing that she would need you can  see her influence by comparing shot  coverage in the Breakfast Club with  Hughes his previous film sixteen Candles  for example both films had a weird girl  listening in on the main conversation  but in sixteen Candles she's only filmed  in the wide shot and because of that  limitation her performance doesn't  really land in her scene with the better  shot coverage in the Breakfast Club Dede  Allen had much more to work with like  her previous work The Breakfast Club is  a feat in editing Allen cuts between up  to six characters at once and it never  feels confusing or unnatural this film  prove